## Acceptance and mechanism

- Cargo.toml:12 — adds only the bboxy-firehose workspace member. Cargo.lock:120 — adds its package entry; no existing dependency versions changed.
- crates/bboxy-firehose/Cargo.toml:1 — one release CLI; libc is its only runtime dependency. bbt-core and serde_json are dev-only.
- crates/bboxy-firehose/src/lib.rs:18 — ten include_bytes! scene assets; src/lib.rs:72 — frozen include_bytes! screen-hash manifest. Playback reads no input/files or terminal size.
- corpus/generate.py:1 — author-time synthetic corpus construction. Original Claude 482-byte and 303-byte captures are embedded verbatim. All ten original Codex captures are zero bytes; no real Codex state is claimed. tests/corpus.rs:74 checks provenance and every empty capture.
- src/options.rs:10 — positive byte and LF-line rate caps, max, explicit units and overflow rejection. src/options.rs:68 — scene/list/check, real/flood, seed and infinite or bounded loop options.
- src/lib.rs:89 — deterministic seeded scene permutation; src/main.rs:122 — deterministic real burst/pause schedule and cumulative rate deadlines. A fixed named scene has fixed bytes for every seed.
- src/main.rs:96 — prebuilt roughly 1 MiB batch for max/flood; bounded memory for infinite output. src/main.rs:21 — signal request atomics; restoration outside the handler. Tests verify SIGINT restores terminal modes promptly and a closed pipe stops an infinite flood.
- src/main.rs:69 — --check prints frozen 80x24 semantic screen expectations without parsing or emitting ANSI. README.md documents the exact FNV-1a/JSON schema. tests/corpus.rs:47 verifies all ten expectations across 5 chunk sizes (50 cases).
- tests/cli.rs — ten CLI tests; tests/corpus.rs — three corpus/provenance/effect tests. t1-validation.log records cargo test: 13 passed; fmt, strict clippy, release build and whitespace check: all exit 0.
- tests/throughput.py — actual release CLI, --rate max --loop 200000000 > /dev/null. t1-throughput.log: 2,434,800,000,000 accepted bytes / 4.489680375 s = 517,187.4235 MiB/s; binary exit 0, measurement exit 0. Requirement >=200 MiB/s holds. This measures /dev/null write acceptance, not PTY/parser/GPU throughput.

